<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><channel><title>Ssh on KevCaz's Website</title><link>https://kevcaz.github.io/tags/ssh/</link><description>Recent content in Ssh on KevCaz's Website</description><generator>Hugo</generator><language>en-us</language><copyright>Content under CC0 1.0 Universal unless otherwise specified.</copyright><lastBuildDate>Tue, 11 Jan 2022 00:00:00 +0000</lastBuildDate><atom:link href="https://kevcaz.github.io/tags/ssh/index.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><title>Basic Git server on my Raspberry Pi</title><link>https://kevcaz.github.io/notes/2022/01/2022-01-11_basic_git_server/</link><pubDate>Tue, 11 Jan 2022 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://kevcaz.github.io/notes/2022/01/2022-01-11_basic_git_server/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;As mentioned in my &lt;a href="../../notes/rapsberrypi/nextcloud_server/"&gt;previous post&lt;/a&gt;, I have recently started using my Raspberry Pi as a server and I decided to try to host a basic Git server.&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;p&gt;As explained in the chapter &lt;a href="https://git-scm.com/book/en/v2/Git-on-the-Server-The-Protocols"&gt;&amp;ldquo;Git on the Server&amp;rdquo;&lt;/a&gt; of Pro Git&lt;sup id="fnref:1"&gt;&lt;a href="#fn:1" class="footnote-ref" role="doc-noteref"&gt;1&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/sup&gt;, among other protocols, Git supports SSH and it is quite straightforward to setup a basic Git server via this protocol. As I can access my Raspberry Pi via ssh, I decided to give it a try! I first connected to it as user &lt;code&gt;pi&lt;/code&gt; (&lt;code&gt;ssh pi@raspberry.local&lt;/code&gt;), a user that is listed as a super user, and I created a new user: &lt;code&gt;git&lt;/code&gt; (this is one way suggested in Pro Git).&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Generate a ssh key with R</title><link>https://kevcaz.github.io/notes/2020/04/2020-04-04_generate_a_ssh/</link><pubDate>Sat, 04 Apr 2020 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://kevcaz.github.io/notes/2020/04/2020-04-04_generate_a_ssh/</guid><description>&lt;p&gt;I now frequently use &lt;a href="https://github.com/features/actions"&gt;GitHub actions&lt;/a&gt; to
deploy websites on gh-pages. One way to do so requires to use a &lt;a href="https://help.github.com/en/github/authenticating-to-github/generating-a-new-ssh-key-and-adding-it-to-the-ssh-agent#generating-a-new-ssh-key"&gt;ssh
key&lt;/a&gt;
(the full procedure is well explained, &lt;em&gt;inter alia&lt;/em&gt;, in the README of
&lt;a href="https://github.com/peaceiris/actions-gh-pages"&gt;peaceiris/actions-gh-pages&lt;/a&gt;). As
I am used to work with the Terminal, when I need such a key, I use the following
command line:&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;div class="highlight"&gt;&lt;div style="color:#575279;background-color:#faf4ed;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;-webkit-text-size-adjust:none;"&gt;
&lt;table style="border-spacing:0;padding:0;margin:0;border:0;"&gt;&lt;tr&gt;&lt;td style="vertical-align:top;padding:0;margin:0;border:0;"&gt;
&lt;pre tabindex="0" style="color:#575279;background-color:#faf4ed;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;-webkit-text-size-adjust:none;"&gt;&lt;code&gt;&lt;span style="white-space:pre;-webkit-user-select:none;user-select:none;margin-right:0.4em;padding:0 0.4em 0 0.4em;color:#7f7f7f"&gt;1
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/td&gt;
&lt;td style="vertical-align:top;padding:0;margin:0;border:0;;width:100%"&gt;
&lt;pre tabindex="0" style="color:#575279;background-color:#faf4ed;-moz-tab-size:4;-o-tab-size:4;tab-size:4;-webkit-text-size-adjust:none;"&gt;&lt;code class="language-sh" data-lang="sh"&gt;&lt;span style="display:flex;"&gt;&lt;span&gt;$ ssh-keygen -t rsa -b &lt;span style="color:#ea9d34"&gt;4096&lt;/span&gt; -C &lt;span style="color:#ea9d34"&gt;&amp;#34;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#286983"&gt;$(&lt;/span&gt;git config user.email&lt;span style="color:#286983"&gt;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;span style="color:#ea9d34"&gt;&amp;#34;&lt;/span&gt; -f gh-pages -N &lt;span style="color:#ea9d34"&gt;&amp;#34;&amp;#34;&lt;/span&gt;
&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/code&gt;&lt;/pre&gt;&lt;/td&gt;&lt;/tr&gt;&lt;/table&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;&lt;p&gt;which generates two files:&lt;/p&gt;</description></item></channel></rss>